  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 531 AM MDT Fri Aug 28 2026/ \n\nClosed upper level low over Gulf of Alaska and associated upper \nlevel trough over the Eastern Pacific will dig south to over the \nPacific Northwest by Saturday morning before lifting and pivoting \neast northeast over the Northern Rockies through the day on Sunday. \nSouthwesterly flow aloft ahead of the approaching upper level trough \nwill continue to transport Pacific moisture north and east over the \nNorthern Rockies through the timeframe, which combined with surface \nheating during the daylight hours and subtle waves lifting northeast \nwithin the overall flow will support showers and thunderstorms \nacross portions of Southwest through North Central Montana. While an \nisolated strong thunderstorm can't be ruled out through Sunday, \nespecially on today and Saturday, most thunderstorms will be of the \ngarden variety and produce gusty and erratic winds. High \ntemperatures in the upper 70s to upper 80s today will fall to the \n60s to low 70s by Sunday as the upper level trough moves overhead, \nwith increasing west winds during the day on Sunday.\n\nAn active pattern looks to stay in place across the Western CONUS \nbeyond Sunday as longwave troughing looks to remain over the Eastern \nPacific, with southwesterly flow over the Northern Rockies helping \nto maintain near to below normal temperatures and daily chances for \nshowers and storms. - Moldan \n\n- Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nStrong and Gusty Winds on Sunday...\n\nClimate anomaly indicators are suggesting an unusual, with respect \nto late August wind climatology, wind event along the Rocky Mountain \nFront as cross barrier flow increases in wake of a departing upper \nlevel wave. H700 flow per NAEFS analysis is prog'd to increase to \nbetween 40-50kts along the Rocky Mountain Front during the day on \nSunday, which is 1 to nearly 2 standard deviations above normal for \nthis timeframe. These values combined with deep mixing during the \nafternoon hours on Sunday in wake of the departing upper level wave \nwill help to support breezy and gusty winds for most locations \nacross the plains of Central and North Central Montana, most notably \nalong and west of the I-15 corridor where NBM5.0 probabilities \ncurrently support a 90% or greater chance for wind gusts to exceed \n35 mph, with the exception of the Southern High Plains zone where \nprobabilities generally range from a 60-75% chance. The table below \nlists the NBM5.0 probabilities for wind gusts to exceed 45, 55 and \n65 mph during the day on Sunday.\n\n                     Sunday's Wind Gusts Probabilities\nLOCATION               > 45 mph | > 55 mph | > 65 mph\nBabb                      60%   |    20%   |     5%\nEast Glacier              70%   |    20%   |     5%\nBrowning                  70%   |    15%   |     0% \nCut Bank                  70%   |    20%   |    <5%\nShelby                    30%   |    <5%   |     0%\nHeart Butte               75%   |    25%   |     5%  \nChoteau                   10%   |    <5%   |     0% \nAugusta                   20%   |    <5%   |     0%  \n\nLight Mountain Snowfall on Sunday...\n\nSnow levels are expected to fall to as low as 7500ft during the day \non Sunday across the East Glacier Park Region as the closed low \nassociated with the upper level trough pivots overhead. These \nlowered snow level would result in some mountain passes in the \nbackcountry of Glacier National Park potentially seeing some minor \nsnowfall, with the latest NBM5.0 probabilities indicating a 10-20% \nchance for snowfall accumulations to exceed 0.1\" during the day on \nSunday. What remains uncertain with respect to Sunday is how much \nprecipitation will be left over across the Northern Rockies as snow \nlevels fall to their minimums for the day; with latest guidance \nsuggesting that most if not all of the precipitation will fall prior \nto snow levels \"bottoming out\", which would result in little to no \nsnowfall overall. - Moldan",
